## libarchive

Grape reads RAR archives with `bsdtar` from [libarchive](https://www.libarchive.org/) 3.8.10, built by Grape's CI from the release tarball with six small patches (three upstream fixes and three of Grape's own). It ships as a separate program: `bsdtar.exe` next to `Grape.exe` on Windows, and `Grape.app/Contents/MacOS/bsdtar` on macOS. Its license texts ship with it in the `licenses/libarchive` folder, where `SOURCE.md` says exactly how it was built.

| Component | License | Text in `licenses/libarchive` |
|---|---|---|
| libarchive and bsdtar | BSD-2-Clause | `LICENSE.txt`, with the copyright holders in `copyright-lines.txt` |
| Parts from the University of California (compress, strmode, umask, setmode) | BSD-3-Clause | `regents-bsd-3-clause.txt` |
| Parts from The NetBSD Foundation (red-black tree, device numbers) | BSD-2-Clause | `netbsd-bsd-2-clause.txt` |
| Windows error-code table, from PostgreSQL (Windows build only) | PostgreSQL License | `postgresql.txt` |
| BLAKE2 | CC0-1.0, which Grape takes (or OpenSSL, or Apache-2.0) | `blake2.txt` |
| Unicode composition tables, from Unicode 6.0 data | Unicode-3.0 | `unicode.txt` |
| arc4random and readpassphrase, from OpenBSD (used only where the system has none, so not normally in the Windows or macOS build) | ISC | `openbsd-isc.txt` |
| Branch filters from XZ Embedded and XZ Utils, in the 7-Zip reader | 0BSD | none needed |
| PPMd (Igor Pavlov, Dmitry Shkarin) and the date parser | Public domain | none needed |

This section is written by hand: check it again when `scripts/bsdtar/sources.json` changes libarchive's version.

## FFmpeg and ffprobe

Grape ships FFmpeg, which converts the audio and video, and ffprobe, which reads media details. They aren't compiled into Grape: they're separate programs next to it (`ffmpeg.exe` and `ffprobe.exe` next to `Grape.exe` on Windows, `ffmpeg` and `ffprobe` in `Grape.app/Contents/MacOS` on macOS), and Grape starts them when it needs them. Grape's CI builds them from FFmpeg 9.0.2 with only the parts Grape uses, and without any networking code. They're under the GNU General Public License, version 2 or later (GPL-2.0-or-later), because they include x264.

Their license texts ship with them in the `licenses/ffmpeg` folder: in Grape's install folder on Windows, and in `Grape.app/Contents/Resources/licenses/ffmpeg/` on macOS. `SOURCE.md` there gives the exact versions, the configure line, and where to get the complete source: `grape-ffmpeg-sources.tar`, on the GitHub Release that ships the installer and in the CI run that built them.

MP3 encoding uses LAME: https://lame.sourceforge.io/

This software is based in part on the work of the Independent JPEG Group. (FFmpeg's DCT code: its notice is in `licenses/ffmpeg/ffmpeg-notices.txt`.)

These are built into them. Each has its own text in `licenses/ffmpeg`.

| Part | Version | License (SPDX) | Text |
|---|---|---|---|
| FFmpeg | 9.0.2 | GPL-2.0-or-later in this build | `LICENSE.txt`, `ffmpeg-LICENSE.md` |
| x264 | stable branch, commit `b35605ac` | GPL-2.0-or-later | `x264.txt` |
| LAME | 4.0 | LGPL-2.0-or-later | `lame.txt`, `lame-LICENSE.txt` |
| Opus | 1.6.1 | BSD-3-Clause | `opus.txt` |
| libvorbis | 1.3.7 | BSD-3-Clause | `vorbis.txt` |
| libogg | 1.3.6 | BSD-3-Clause | `ogg.txt` |
| libvpx | 1.17.0 | BSD-3-Clause | `libvpx.txt`, `libvpx-PATENTS.txt`, `libvpx-x86inc.txt` |
| dav1d | 1.5.4 | BSD-2-Clause | `dav1d.txt`, `dav1d-PATENTS.txt` |
| zlib | 1.3.2 | Zlib | `zlib.txt` |
| Code from other projects inside FFmpeg and the libraries above | | BSD-2-Clause, BSD-2-Clause-Views, BSD-3-Clause, MIT, ISC, Zlib, IJG, BSL-1.0, TU-Berlin-1.0 | `ffmpeg-notices.txt`, `dsd2pcm.txt`, `sha2-gifford.txt`, `glumpy.txt`, `libwebp.txt` |
| mingw-w64 runtime (Windows) | 11.0.1 | ZPL-2.1, with the notices in its text | `mingw-w64-runtime.txt` |
| GCC runtime, libgcc (Windows) | 13.2.0 | GPL-3.0-or-later WITH GCC-exception-3.1 | `gcc-runtime-exception.txt`, `LICENSE-GPLv3.txt` |

Some of their source files hold code from other projects, like MIPS's fixed-point code, WebRTC's iLBC decoder, musl's `sscanf` and x86inc.asm. `ffmpeg-notices.txt` has each of those notices verbatim, with the files it's in; Grape's build checks that it has every one in what it compiles. Where a file only names the license of code it took from another project, that project's license text is there too: dsd2pcm's (FFmpeg's DSD decoder), Aaron D. Gifford's (its SHA-2 code), Glumpy's (its scaler's filters) and libwebp's (libvpx's threads). libvorbis's LPC code carries Jutta Degener and Carsten Bormann's terms (TU-Berlin-1.0). libvpx and dav1d come with patent grants, the `-PATENTS.txt` files. The GCC Runtime Library Exception lets libgcc's code be distributed under any terms, so it doesn't change the programs' license. `grape-ffmpeg-sources.tar` also holds the mingw-w64 runtime's source.

## grape-heif (HEIC and AVIF on Windows)

Grape converts HEIC and AVIF images on Windows with `grape-heif.exe`, a small program of Grape's own (`tools/grape-heif`) that Grape's CI builds. It's a separate program next to `Grape.exe`, which Grape starts for each HEIC or AVIF image; none of it is inside Grape itself. It links the libraries below statically, and because x265 is under the GPL, the program as a whole is under the **GNU General Public License, version 3 or later**. Its license texts ship with it in the `licenses/grape-heif` folder, where `SOURCE.md` says exactly how it was built. On macOS Grape uses the system's ImageIO instead, and rav1e (its own section, below) to write AVIF, so the Mac build has no grape-heif.

| Component | License | Text in `licenses/grape-heif` |
|---|---|---|
| grape-heif.exe as a whole | GPL-3.0-or-later | `LICENSE.txt` |
| libheif 1.23.7 | LGPL-3.0-or-later | `libheif.txt` |
| libde265 1.1.3 | LGPL-3.0-or-later, and the DOC (ACE/TAO) terms for its Windows condition-variable code | `libde265.txt`, `libde265-win32cond.txt` |
| x265 4.3 | GPL-2.0-or-later | `x265.txt` |
| dav1d 1.5.4 (AVIF decoding) | BSD-2-Clause, its x86 assembly's ISC notice, and the Alliance for Open Media Patent License 1.0 | `dav1d.txt`, `dav1d-x86inc.txt`, `dav1d-PATENTS.txt` |
| libaom 3.15.2 (its AVIF encoder only) | BSD-2-Clause, with fastfeat (BSD-3-Clause), vector (MIT) and x86inc (ISC), and the Alliance for Open Media Patent License 1.0 | `aom.txt` |
| The Rust crates in it (libheif-rs, libheif-sys, serde and others) | MIT, Apache-2.0 and Unicode-3.0 | `rust-crates.md` |
| Grape's own code in it | MIT | Grape's `LICENSE` |
| The Visual C++ runtime, linked statically | Visual Studio's redistribution terms, which need no notice | none needed |

**The source.** Every build of the installer comes with grape-heif's complete source, `grape-heif-1.23.7-grape.2-source.tar.gz`, on the GitHub Release that ships the installer and in the CI run that built it. It holds Grape's code for the program, every Rust crate it uses, the five libraries' source archives, vcpkg's build recipes and how to build it again.

This section is written by hand: check it again when `tools/grape-heif/vcpkg.lock.json` changes a version.

## rav1e (AVIF on macOS)

Grape for macOS writes AVIF images with [rav1e](https://crates.io/crates/rav1e/0.8.1) 0.8.1, an AV1 encoder, through [ravif](https://crates.io/crates/ravif/0.13.0) 0.13.0. Both are compiled into Grape itself on macOS; Grape for Windows doesn't have them. rav1e and two crates it's built from, v_frame and av1-grain, are under the BSD 2-Clause License and the Alliance for Open Media Patent License 1.0. Their BSD 2-Clause texts are with the Rust crates below. The patent license, and the notices of the code rav1e took from libaom and dav1d, are here.

### Code from libaom and dav1d

Some of rav1e's Rust code comes from libaom, the Alliance for Open Media's AV1 library. On Apple silicon, Grape also builds in rav1e's arm64 assembly: one of its files comes partly from libaom, and most of the others from dav1d. The Intel build has none of the assembly.

The code from libaom is under the BSD 2-Clause License text in rav1e's `LICENSE`, which is with the Rust crates below, and the patent license further down. Its notices:

